what-people-say.content:
When the flyers go up, everyone suddenly has a story about the Witchlight Carnival. Some of these are true. Some are half true. Some are what happens when a tale is told by a grandmother, then a tavern, then a very small cousin.



                  &quot;It comes every eight years, regular as a comet. Nobody has ever seen it arrive. One evening the field is empty, and the next there are tents.&quot; 






                  &quot;The owners are two elves, Witch and Light. My father saw them when he was a boy. He swears they haven&#039;t aged a day.&quot; 






                  &quot;Buy a ticket, don&#039;t sneak in. My gran says it&#039;s bad manners. My gran says a lot of things.&quot; 






                  &quot;Whatever you&#039;ve lost, you&#039;ll find it at the Witchlight, if you know where to look.&quot; 






                  &quot;The calliope plays all night long, and there&#039;s never anyone sitting at the keys.&quot; 






                  &quot;Old Tam says there&#039;s a tent in there that doesn&#039;t lead back out, only somewhere else entirely. Old Tam also says he once married a mermaid.&quot; 






                  &quot;The snail races are rigged. The fastest snail is a wizard. Everybody knows that.&quot; 






                  &quot;The candyfloss is made from real clouds. That&#039;s why it never rains while the carnival&#039;s in town.&quot; 






                  &quot;Half the folk who work there aren&#039;t from this world. That&#039;s where the strange ones come from: other worlds, other skies.&quot; 






                  &quot;Don&#039;t laugh at the clowns. Or do laugh at the clowns. I can never remember which one gets you into trouble.&quot; 






                  &quot;Never tell the carnival folk your real name. My aunt told one of them hers was Duchess Marigold of the Seven Hills. It&#039;s Brenda.&quot; 






                  &quot;If one of the carnival folk gives you something, give something back. Doesn&#039;t matter what. Uncle Bert gave one a sock, and he&#039;s been lucky ever since.&quot; 






                  &quot;Be polite in there. Rude guests get turned into things. Toads, mostly.&quot; 






                  &quot;Don&#039;t eat anything at the carnival. Except the cake. No, wait: especially not the cake.&quot; 






                  &quot;Cousin Wim went to the carnival for one night and came home a week late. That&#039;s his story, anyway. His wife has her own.&quot; 






                  &quot;Don&#039;t still be inside when the sun comes up. Or you&#039;ll leave with it.&quot; 



Which of these are true? You’ll have to go and see.
